<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Skanderbeg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1405">1405</a>–<a href="/years/1468">1468</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Gjergj Kastrioti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Skanderbeg, born Gjergj Kastrioti, was an Albanian lord who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and in 1443 broke with them and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a>. In 1444 he formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a> with other Albanian nobles and for twenty-five years resisted Ottoman campaigns, including sieges of Krujë. Albania fell to the Ottomans within a decade of his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skanderbeg.jpg" alt="Skanderbeg" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Skanderbeg_(1466).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Skanderbeg, born Gjergj Kastrioti, belonged to the Kastrioti family. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-kastrioti">Gjon Kastrioti</a>, held territory between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> that included Mat, Mirditë and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diber">Dibër</a> in north-central Albania. The origin of his mother Voisava is disputed. His birthplace is also debated, though a birth year of 1405 is now largely agreed. He was sent as a hostage to the Ottoman court at Adrianople in 1415 and again in 1423, and received military training at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enderun-school">Enderun School</a>. He served the sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> for about twenty years and was appointed sanjakbey of the Sanjak of Dibra in 1440.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the Battle of Nish in 1443 Skanderbeg deserted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and became ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a> and nearby areas. In March 1444 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a> with local noblemen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> at first supported him as a buffer against the Ottomans, but a dispute over the fortress of Dagnum led to an Albanian–Venetian war in 1447–1448, in which Venice offered rewards for his assassination. In June 1450 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> besieged Krujë with an army of about 100,000. Skanderbeg left a garrison of 1,500 under Vrana Konti and harassed the Ottoman supply caravans, and the garrison repelled three major assaults.</p><p>By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> of 26 March 1451 he recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of Alfonso V of Aragon over his lands in exchange for military aid, though he remained in practice an independent ruler. In 1460 he sent cavalry to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferdinand I of Naples</a> against an Angevin revolt. In 1463 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> intended him as chief commander of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, but the Pope died while the armies were gathering. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> alongside the Venetians until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Skanderbeg died on 17 January 1468. After his death and the Ottoman conquest of his lands, many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> found refuge in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, which contributed to the formation of the Arbëresh communities of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> saw him as a model of Christian resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and he became a central figure of the Albanian National Awakening of the 19th century and is the national hero of Albania. Historians note that none of his victories permanently stopped the Ottomans from invading the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Skanderbeg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the besieging Ottoman army defeated, <b>Skanderbeg</b>, leader of the League of Lezhë, entered his main fortress of Krujë, which had held out since June 1466.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-23">23 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Leading the League of Lezhë, <b>Skanderbeg</b> defeated an Ottoman army under Ali Pasha at Torvioll in Albania, attacking its flanks and rear with troops hidden in the woods.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-29">29 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Venetian patronage, <b>Skanderbeg</b> summoned Albanian noblemen to Lezhë, where they formed a military alliance against the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>An Ottoman army led by Sultan Murad II and his crown prince Mehmed II besieged Krujë, the Albanian capital of <b>Skanderbeg</b>'s League of Lezhë.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-14">14 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Having deserted the Ottoman army, <b>Skanderbeg</b> arrived at Krujë and, using a forged letter from Sultan Murad II to its governor, became lord of the city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Skanderbeg</b> and the Republic of Venice signed a peace negotiated by Georgius Pelino.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sinë, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.73,20.3&amp;year=1445" aria-label="Sinë on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5044 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Skanderbeg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5494 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5494 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.73,20.3&amp;year=1445">What was here in 1445? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
